{
  "image_name": "ea31625ad9ab694bd5c6e24aedb4c7cc.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes three main biological responses to climate change and the genomic tools used to study them. At the center, climate change is shown as a driver that can lead to extirpation or extinction if organisms fail to respond appropriately, with paleogenomics and ancient DNA indicated as ways to investigate those losses.\n\nOne response is genetic adaptation. The left panel shows populations in different environments experiencing spatially varying selection, producing genetic change tied to local conditions, and temporally varying selection, where changing conditions over time cause selective mortality and shifts in which genotypes persist. This highlights evolution as a route to persistence.\n\nA second response is range shifts and dispersal. The upper-right panel depicts species moving from poor to healthier environments, including possible species relocation, and shows that environmental change can also alter community composition. Methods such as eDNA metabarcoding, ecological niche modelling, and metagenomics are listed as ways to track these patterns.\n\nThe third response is phenotypic plasticity. The lower panel emphasizes molecular mechanisms like gene expression and epigenetics that allow acclimation without genetic change, although plasticity may also be insufficient, leading to mortality. Overall, the figure presents climate change as filtering populations through adaptation, movement, or plastic responses.",
